==Latin==

===Etymology===
{{etymon|la|id=give|:inh|itc-pro:*didō<id:give>}}
From {{inh|la|itc-pro|*didō}}, from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*dédeh₃ti}}, from the root {{m|ine-pro|*deh₃-||give}}. The reduplication was lost in Latin in the present tense, but is preserved in the perfect tense and in other Italic languages. A root aorist (from {{cog|ine-pro|*déh₃t}}) is preserved in Venetic {{m|xve|𐌆𐌏𐌕𐌏}}; the other Italic perfect forms reflect a reduplicated stative, {{m|itc-pro||*dedai}}. However, the root aorist possibly served as the source of the Latin present forms.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=dō, dāre|pages=174-175}}</ref>

The form {{m|la|duim}} may reflect earlier {{der|la|itc-pro||*dū-ī-}}, from the optative of an older {{der|la|ine-pro|-}} aorist {{m|ine-pro||*déwh₃-t}}. The stem of this aorist perhaps derives the [[metathesis]] of {{m|ine-pro||*dh₃u-}}, itself perhaps derives from the root {{m|ine-pro|*deh₃-}} with a u-extension.<ref>{{R:itc:EDL|head=duim|page=182}}</ref> Sihler, however, dismisses the proposed root extension as "[[ad hoc]]" and arguing that the proposed cognates, {{m|grc|δοϝέναι}} and {{m|sa|दावने|tr=dāváne}}, have more plausible explanations within their own branches, thus leaving the Italic forms isolated.<ref>{{R:Sihler 1995|page=545}}</ref> Another theory, advanced by the linguist {{w|Jay H. Jasanoff}}, suggests that the form derives from {{inh|la|itc-pro|*dowjō|*dōwīm}}, itself from {{inh|la|ine-pro|*deh₃-|*déh₃-ih₁-m̥}}, with a glide inserted to break the hiatus.<ref>{{R:la:OHCGL|page=417}}</ref> The term escaped the effects of Thurneysen-Havet's Law due to the root accent.<ref>{{R:itc:Vine:2006|page=240}}</ref>

Cognates include {{cog|grc|δίδωμι}}, {{cog|sa|ददाति|tr=dádāti}}, {{cog|peo|𐎭𐎭𐎠𐎬𐎢𐎺}}.

The derivatives of {{m|la||dō}} are not always easy to distinguish from those of {{m|la|-dō|t=put}} < {{m|ine-pro|*dʰeh₁-}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{la-IPA|dō}}

===Verb===
